      <h3><span style="margin-right: 0.4rem;">💬</span> Reflection Prompts</h3>

<p>Each unit includes a reflection prompt — a question or scenario that asks you to think carefully about what you've read and form your own reasoned response. These take the place of traditional discussion boards.</p>

<p>Reflection prompts are designed to push your thinking beyond summary. You're not just recounting what a philosopher said — you're engaging with the argument, testing it against your own reasoning, and articulating a position you can defend.</p>

<h4>How to approach them</h4>
<ul>
<li>Read the prompt carefully and make sure you understand what's being asked</li>
<li>Take time to think before you write — a good philosophical response isn't rushed</li>
<li>Support your position with reasoning and evidence from the texts, not just personal opinion</li>
<li>Consider potential objections to your view and address them</li>
<li>Aim for clarity over length — a focused, well-argued paragraph is worth more than a rambling page</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>These are for your benefit.</strong> Reflection prompts are a space for you to practice thinking and writing philosophically. Treat them as an opportunity to develop your ideas, not as busy work.</p>
</div></div>
