      <h3><span style="margin-right: 0.4rem;">✍️</span> Final Essay</h3>

<p>At the end of the course, you'll write a <strong>final essay</strong> on a philosopher of your choice. This is your chance to go deeper into the thinker whose ideas resonated with you most (or provoked you most — that works too).</p>

<h4>What's expected</h4>
<ul>
<li>Choose one of the six philosophers covered in the course</li>
<li>Develop a focused argument or analysis related to their work</li>
<li>Minimum three pages, MLA format</li>
<li>Support your claims with evidence from the primary texts we've read</li>
</ul>

<h4>Getting started early</h4>
<p>You don't need to wait until the end of the course to start thinking about your essay. As you work through each unit, pay attention to which ideas stick with you, which arguments you find most compelling (or most flawed), and which philosopher's way of thinking feels most relevant. Those instincts are often the seed of a good paper.</p>

<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Choose your philosopher wisely.</strong> You'll be spending several pages with their ideas — pick someone whose thinking genuinely interests or challenges you.</p>
</div></div>
